# gitleaks fingerprint allowlist.
#
# ONE entry, and it should stay that way. A fingerprint pins a specific finding
# to a specific commit, file, rule and line, so it cannot silently widen into
# "stop checking this rule" or "stop checking this file" the way a config
# exclusion would. If a new finding appears, it is a new finding — fix it in the
# code rather than adding a line here.
#
# Why this file exists at all
#
# ci.yml scans with `--log-opts=--all` on pushes to main, which walks all ~275
# commits — including the upstream history this fork inherited from
# brianshea2/addr.tools, which we do not control and cannot rewrite. Every push
# to main has failed on a single finding in upstream's own initial public
# commit (2024-05-06), long before the fork existed.
#
# The finding is a false positive, and upstream says so in the line directly
# above it:
#
#     // not really a secret, published for client <...>, not enforced
#     TsigSecret = "<...>"
#
# It is a deliberately published, unenforced TSIG value for a public test zone.
# It is also already public in upstream's repository, so nothing is being
# concealed by this entry that is not already on GitHub.
#
# The file was deleted upstream in d155e79 ("remove discontinued zone handlers")
# and does not exist in this tree. `gitleaks dir .` — the working tree, no
# history — reports no leaks. So the CURRENT code is clean by the same tool;
# only the archaeology is red.
#
# What this entry does NOT do: it does not weaken scanning of anything we write.
# The rule stays enabled, the file glob stays unrestricted, and any new
# occurrence of a real credential — including a re-introduction of this exact
# string in a live file — produces a different fingerprint and still fails.
#
# Format: <commit>:<path>:<rule-id>:<start-line>
